{"h":3395,"lines":[{"x":0.20987654274730427,"y":0.10600706727222098,"w":0.057613169352213556,"h":0.017667844522968212,"c":1,"t":"Test 4"},{"w":0.11739152272542319,"y":0.15094300342705391,"c":1,"t":"READING","x":0.5143495058789066,"h":0.014974458197963281},{"x":0.20987653980954643,"y":0.20053003548510362,"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","h":0.018946708301773363,"w":0.27160494486490883,"c":1},{"w":0.71241861979166665,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","x":0.20960742492990686,"y":0.23173153341280117,"h":0.023486372158018964,"c":1},{"t":"Passage 1 below.","x":0.2077457459305648,"y":0.24823522327882885,"w":0.14623690287272137,"h":0.018064436558699826,"c":1},{"y":0.28015453194137796,"t":"The secret of staying young","h":0.02706858519249733,"c":1,"x":0.39450618916347507,"w":0.35513130187988279},{"t":"Pheidole dentata, a native ant of the south-castern U.S., isn't immortal. But scientists have found","y":0.31501750372541926,"w":0.72260533650716141,"h":0.023005971919802137,"c":1,"x":0.20758606546912367},{"y":0.33099035355874618,"x":0.20558459684627622,"t":"that it doesn't seem to show any signs of aging. Old worker ants can do everything just as well as","w":0.72461268107096344,"c":1,"h":0.021214589634548142},{"x":0.20769111004315771,"w":0.72246444702148427,"c":1,"y":0.34763566989892236,"t":"the youngsters, and their brains appear just as sharp. 'We get a picture that these ants really don't","h":0.018139590924985294},{"x":0.20566690383843877,"y":0.36316989847743275,"h":0.019167003698989271,"c":1,"w":0.70799967447916667,"t":"decline,' says Ysabel Giraldo, who studied the ants for her doctoral thesis at Boston University."},{"w":0.67922678629557298,"h":0.017786540468393341,"y":0.39370728676704003,"x":0.20770445846229052,"t":"Such age-defying feats are rare in the animal kingdom. Naked mole rats can live for almost","c":1},{"y":0.40979377944059436,"t":"30 years and stay fit for nearly their entire lives. They can still reproduce even when old, and they","w":0.7244893391927083,"h":0.018611585012734633,"c":1,"x":0.20563947611990899},{"c":1,"x":0.20563795422900494,"h":0.018474367678656844,"y":0.4253940569348823,"t":"never get cancer. But the vast majority of animals deteriorate with age just like people do. Like","w":0.70805145263671876},{"x":0.20564559621696465,"y":0.43996504623551314,"t":"the naked mole rat, ants are social creatures that usually live in highly organised colonies. 'It's","w":0.70186177571614583,"h":0.018776154490045105,"c":1},{"w":0.70591817220052078,"h":0.018221454148579008,"y":0.45621718855977245,"x":0.20363198069657121,"t":"this social complexity that makes P. dentata useful for studying aging in people,' says Giraldo,","c":1},{"y":0.47078380600517522,"w":0.72253326416015629,"c":1,"h":0.020228225294636637,"t":"now at the California Institute of Technology. Humans are also highly social, a trait that has been","x":0.20354142579322751},{"t":"connected to healthier aging. By contrast, most animal studies of aging use mice, worms or fruit","y":0.48655908870150977,"c":1,"h":0.018374774425133689,"w":0.71829930623372396,"x":0.20566415609839567},{"t":"flies, which all lead much more isolated lives.","x":0.20781892876640248,"w":0.33950617472330724,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1,"y":0.50290697661380745},{"x":0.20370370878847741,"y":0.53343023240858467,"w":0.68724278767903646,"c":1,"h":0.016039732066986834,"t":"In the lab, P. dentata worker ants typically live for around 140 days. Giraldo focused on ants"},{"h":0.018779526610537389,"t":"at four age ranges: 20 to 22 days, 45 to 47 days, 95 to 97 days and 120 to 122 days. Unlike all","x":0.20358675209007221,"y":0.54783048877112528,"w":0.69775472005208328,"c":1},{"y":0.56355570893288864,"t":"previous studies, which only estimated how old the ants were, her work tracked the ants from the","x":0.20567199043767781,"w":0.72033808390299481,"h":0.01744163387374964,"c":1},{"w":0.70807525634765622,"h":0.017907138145994983,"c":1,"x":0.20561387901267741,"t":"time the pupae became adults, so she knew their exact ages. Then she put them through a range","y":0.57927193336117311},{"w":0.061698958079020177,"y":0.59345530968583238,"h":0.014565052216690466,"c":1,"t":"of tests.","x":0.20494350223031763},{"h":0.018818931669453143,"y":0.6244973089311554,"w":0.72867273966471358,"c":1,"t":"Giraldo watched how well the ants took care of the young of the colony, recording how often each","x":0.20560047752862032},{"c":1,"y":0.64053744904056342,"t":"ant attended to, carried and fed them. She compared how well 20-day-old and 95-day-old ants","w":0.7018815612792968,"h":0.019326017939159779,"x":0.20152355365932711},{"c":1,"x":0.20356924943116325,"w":0.69776880900065097,"t":"followed the telltale scent that the insects usually leave to mark a trail to food. She tested how","y":0.65575432691978897,"h":0.018827716930735483},{"h":0.016841268370935025,"x":0.2036266712135901,"y":0.67177242859105502,"w":0.71208882649739591,"c":1,"t":"ants responded to light and also measured how active they were by counting how often ants in a"},{"h":0.017209366999469955,"w":0.72653650919596346,"t":"small dish walked across a line. And she experimented with how ants react to live prey: a tethered","x":0.20360451667362195,"y":0.68685417565758389,"c":1},{"t":"fruit fly. Giraldo expected the older ants to perform poorly in all these tasks. But the elderly","w":0.68140309651692721,"h":0.019719670883196705,"c":1,"x":0.20148630176947477,"y":0.70183233947616619},{"t":"insects were all good caretakers and trail-followers-the 95-day-old ants could track the scent","c":1,"h":0.015677462197024039,"y":0.71799583213665807,"x":0.20359196037776001,"w":0.69773173014322931},{"h":0.017947463180488121,"x":0.20359761410994545,"w":0.71009348551432294,"c":1,"y":0.73280599677432379,"t":"even longer than their younger counterparts. They all responded to light well, and the older ants"},{"c":1,"t":"were more active. And when it came to reacting to prey, the older ants attacked the poor fruit fly","x":0.20361481788710808,"y":0.74853765146192774,"w":0.71212341308593741,"h":0.017604076398977542},{"y":0.76386991674856086,"x":0.19944898112498019,"t":"just as aggressively as the young ones did, flaring their mandibles or pulling at the fly's legs.","h":0.018904725289316637,"c":1,"w":0.68544947306315096},{"y":0.79323792119607406,"x":0.20143450081323047,"c":1,"h":0.022069737824169322,"w":0.7081885782877605,"t":"Then Giraldo compared the brains of 20-day-old and 95-day-old ants, identifying any cells that"},{"x":0.20364329075271598,"w":0.70176488240559887,"h":0.015815503465272829,"t":"were close to death. She saw no major differences with age, nor was there any difference in the","c":1,"y":0.81058192825952724},{"y":0.82545840865718856,"w":0.70797592163085932,"c":1,"h":0.018140601325512429,"x":0.1995008268130013,"t":"location of the dying cells, showing that age didn't seem to affect specific brain functions. Ants"},{"t":"and other insects have structures in their brains called mushroom bodies, which are important for","y":0.84142341185326075,"c":1,"h":0.01807960099129291,"x":0.19952135340741156,"w":0.72645512898763021},{"x":0.19958847746941524,"t":"82","w":0.024691357612609877,"y":0.86482558121238806,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1}],"w":2400}